/*
================================================================================
RMSTEST_74 - other_zone_avoidance_distance: origin-spacing, but with enough
statistical power to mean something
docs/known-issues.md BUG-016 (reopened), preview-design Sec.6.1, lands.ts
(neutralOrigin, the ORIGIN_ATTEMPTS/minPlacementDistance loop), supersedes
RMSTEST_71's origin-spacing read
================================================================================

THE QUESTION
RMSTEST_71 tried to show that a randomly-placed origin keeps
other_zone_avoidance_distance away from a prior origin, using one big WALL
land and six small satellites checking against it (plus each other). Every
one of those 21 checks (6 vs the wall, 15 pairs among the six) came back
clean - no violation - and that was read as confirming the mechanism works.

IT DOES NOT SETTLE ANYTHING. A 15-tile Chebyshev exclusion zone around one
point covers a 31x31 = 961-tile square on a 200x200 (40000-tile) map: about
2.4% of the map. Even with ZERO enforcement, a single random origin has a
97.6% chance of landing outside any one exclusion zone by pure luck.
RMSTEST_71's 21 checks give an expected chance-collision count of only
~0.5 under the null (no enforcement at all) - so a clean run had roughly a
1-in-5 to 1-in-3 chance of happening by accident even if the attribute does
nothing whatsoever for origin placement. RMSTEST_71's result is therefore
UNDECIDED, not confirmatory, and needs a properly-powered replacement rather
than a re-read.

METHOD: MANY origins competing for space on ONE map, so a clean pass is
actually improbable under the null. The guide states origin-spacing applies
"regardless of zone", so no wall/backdrop land is needed at all - every land
here just needs to declare the attribute and have no land_position. Twenty
identical SNOW lands, no land_position (fully random origin), small enough
targets (base_size 2, number_of_tiles 50 - a ~5x5 origin stamp plus a little
growth) that each patch's centroid stays close to its true origin, so
patch-to-patch distance is a good proxy for origin-to-origin distance and
growth cannot blur the read the way it did for RMSTEST_71's WALL (which grew
into space its neighbours' origins had correctly kept clear of, per
BUG-016's fixed-up reading of that script - the confound this design removes
by giving every land the SAME small target). Different ZONE per land only so
each is a separate declared command with its own origin roll; the guide says
zone plays no part in this half of the attribute.

STATISTICAL POWER, why 20 and not 6
With n lands all avoiding each other at distance d on a map of area A, the
number of PAIRS checked is C(n,2), each with an approximate per-pair chance
collision probability p = (2d+1)^2 / A under the null. Expected chance
collisions = C(n,2) * p, and P(zero collisions | null) is approximately
exp(-C(n,2)*p) for a Poisson approximation:

  design                    pairs   E[chance collisions]   P(clean | null)
  RMSTEST_71 (6 vs 1 wall)     21           ~0.5                 ~60%
  this script, n=20 d=15      190           ~4.6                  ~1%
  n=25, d=15                  300           ~7.2                ~0.07%

n=20 was chosen over n=25 for placement-packing safety: twenty 31x31
exclusion zones is ~48% of the map's area, comfortably placeable by
rejection sampling; twenty-five approaches 60% and risks originFallbackCenter
fallbacks that would corrupt the read (see STOP RULE below). A clean run at
n=20 is real evidence FOR the mechanism (only ~1% chance of happening by
accident with none); a run with even one or two violations is real evidence
AGAINST it, or of partial/weaker enforcement than 15 tiles.

PREDICTIONS
  ENFORCED    -> --patches SNOW reports 20 separate patches (none merged),
                 and every pairwise centroid distance is >= ~15 (RMSTEST_73
                 calibrated the growth-time half of this attribute at
                 declared_value + 1, so expect a similar +-1 floor here, not
                 exactly 15.0).
  NOT ENFORCED -> some pairs land within 15 tiles of each other by chance
                 (expected ~4.6 out of 190 pairs); with base_size 2 stamps
                 that are only 5 tiles wide, two origins closer than about 5
                 tiles apart will physically overlap/merge into ONE patch
                 under 4-connectivity, so the total patch count dropping
                 below 20 is the single cheapest signal to check first.
  MIXED       -> patch count stays at 20 (nothing merged) but one or more
                 pairwise centroid distances sit well under 15 - the
                 mechanism exists but doesn't reach 15 tiles, or acts as a
                 collision-avoidance floor rather than the declared spacing.

STOP RULE
If the total SNOW tile count is far below the ~1000 expected (20 lands x 50
tiles, plus the usual small-clump overshoot), or if --patches SNOW shows one
patch sitting suspiciously close to the map's exact center relative to the
others, packing pressure forced one or more lands into originFallbackCenter
and the run does not test this question - rerun with n reduced (e.g. 15)
rather than trusting the read.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), ANY PLAYER COUNT. TWO RUNS.
Two runs (not more) because the statistical power argument above already
does the heavy lifting within a single map; a second run is a cheap
reproducibility check in the same spirit as RMSTEST_65's two-run confirmation,
not a contest that needs three like the growth-time scripts.

READ WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches SNOW
  Read the patch COUNT first (20 vs fewer is the headline). Then read the
  centroid list directly from the tool's output and compute all C(20,2)
  pairwise Chebyshev distances by hand or with a short script - the probe
  tool does not do pairwise-among-patches distance itself, only bbox/patch
  reports per terrain.
================================================================================
*/
